$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 05/Temporality

::EPI-W5-037::Why is reverse causation a concern in many cross-sectional studies? {
=Exposure and outcome may be measured simultaneously, obscuring which came first#Correct.
~Cross-sectional studies never measure prevalence#Review the defining design or interpretation logic.
~They always randomise exposure#Review the defining design or interpretation logic.
~They cannot include comparison groups#Review the defining design or interpretation logic.
}

::EPI-W5-038::A cross-sectional survey finds lower physical activity among people with chronic pain. Which alternative explanation is most important before claiming inactivity caused pain? {
=Pain may have reduced physical activity#Correct. The outcome may influence the measured exposure.
~The sample is too large#This is a classic temporal/reverse-causation concern.
~Prevalence cannot be calculated#This is a classic temporal/reverse-causation concern.
~A denominator is never needed#This is a classic temporal/reverse-causation concern.
}

